stion-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-4y=0 \\\\ 4y-5z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-4y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,-4,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y-5z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-5⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =20i+25j+20k \\\\  & =⟨20,25,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,0,-9⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,1,-5⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨20,25,20⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨4,-5,0⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨5,-4,0⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨20,25,20⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "bed14be223a6c812c5876091eb7a34b1f61a32e26594f9fae33a50ea5de21577" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "bed14be223a6c812c5876091eb7a34b1f61a32e26594f9fae33a50ea5de21577" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "54a524d1db17c39bc446363252079dd3624c71e6a1ccee49fb20b1a7421a2edf" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "54a524d1db17c39bc446363252079dd3624c71e6a1ccee49fb20b1a7421a2edf" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "30e47e1674a220532fee83982ff9c230d8cace64a10c6c475367ef03a9dfd222" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "30e47e1674a220532fee83982ff9c230d8cace64a10c6c475367ef03a9dfd222" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-71269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $x=1$ and $y=-z.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $x=1$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $y=-z$ (or $y+z=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,-1,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,-1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,-1,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $z=1$ and $x=y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $z=1$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,0,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x=y$ (or $x-y=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71268" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,1,0⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-4y=0 \\\\ 4y-5z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-4y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,-4,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $4y-5z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-5⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-5⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =20i+25j+20k \\\\  & =⟨20,25,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,0,-9⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,-5⟩"] ["math" "⟨20,25,20⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,-5,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,-4,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71266" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨20,25,20⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3z-11=0 \\\\ 2x-2y-5=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j-4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-129169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-4,6,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨-6,4,-4⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,-6,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨6,-4,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨6,6,-4⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨6,6,-4⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4z-11=0 \\\\ x-2y+31=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-3x+4z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+31=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\times ⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨4,0,3⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,2,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,4,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,8,6⟩"] ["math" "⟨8,4,6⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨8,4,6⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+3y-2z-8=0$ and $2x+y-3z-16=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation gives \n\n$$\nx+0-2z-8=0\\,⟹\\,x=2z+8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=2z+8$ into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2z+8)+0-3z-16 & =0 \\\\ 4z+16-3z-16 & =0 \\\\ z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2(0)+8=8$ and $a=⟨8,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+3y-2z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,3,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y-3z-16=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,3,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-7i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨-7,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨8,0,0⟩+t⟨-7,-1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=8+0=8,$ and $b=⟨-7,-1,-5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-129229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-4,\\,b=⟨-5,-1,7⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-8,\\,b=⟨-7,5,-1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=4,\\,b=⟨-1,-7,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=7,\\,b=⟨-8,-5,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=8,\\,b=⟨-7,-1,-5⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=8,\\,b=⟨-7,-1,-5⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨6,3,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-4,\\,b=⟨9,5,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=2,\\,b=⟨3,9,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=8,\\,b=⟨5,3,9⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4,\\,b=⟨9,3,5⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $-x+y-2z=0$ and $-x-2y+z-3=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n-x+y-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-2y+z-3 & =0 \\\\ -y-2y+0-3 & =0 \\\\ -3y & =3 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y=-1$ and $a=⟨-1,-1,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $-x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-3i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1-1=-2,$ and $b=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1,\\,b=⟨-1,1,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-1,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨-1,1,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-5,\\,b=⟨1,0,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71187" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-2,\\,b=⟨-1,1,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $-x-2y+z=0$ and $2x+4y-2z+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $-x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y-2z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${n}_{2}=-2{n}_{1}.$ So, the normal vectors are parallel.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFor the two planes to coincide, the equation of one plane should be a scalar multiple of the other. However, if we try to manipulate the equation of the second plane to resemble the equation of the first plane, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-2z+1=0 \\\\ x+2y-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ -x-2y+z-\\frac{1}{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, this equation is not the same as the equation of the first plane. So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $2x-y+3z=0$ and $5x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨7,15,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\times ⟨5,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+15j+k \\\\  & =⟨6,15,1⟩\\ne ⟨7,15,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-y+2z=0$ and $5x+y-13=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨-3,12,6⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $5x+y-13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-1,2⟩\\times ⟨5,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2i+10j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,10,6⟩\\ne ⟨-3,12,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-129261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x-y+z=0$ and $x-z+4=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x-y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-z+4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,-1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,4,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "462779e4d414fce3ef4a7d3003258f5132d90dd13e29085ac98aace4b5f8eced"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+5=0 \\\\ 3y-2z-7=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "c282bbd7b2a498567f4b2da6ea954766e1fa169fbed3047a06504a8950574ff1"} {"question" "e-5920" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,0⟩\\times ⟨0,3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,i & \\,j & \\,k\\, \\\\ \\,2 & \\,4 & \\,0\\, \\\\ \\,0 & \\,3 & \\,\\,\\,-2\\,\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i+4j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-8,4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5920.json" "file_sha256" "c282bbd7b2a498567f4b2da6ea954766e1fa169fbed3047a06504a8950574ff1"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3,$ and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "f91c96859f1397262277f8af74ccaffea390a3f79ec352146bfcf3fc801b5848"} {"question" "e-5917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives   \n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)+4(-2z)+2z+18 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,4⟩\\times ⟨2,4,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-12i+6j \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,-6,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, $a_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3,$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle  -2,1,0\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5917.json" "file_sha256" "f91c96859f1397262277f8af74ccaffea390a3f79ec352146bfcf3fc801b5848"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- The planes intersect on a straight line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "c2fd8d995ff794cfc875bf9ad8e8575f8993b719755717eba6c2ab56785f7a78"} {"question" "e-5921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨4,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ n & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However,  the first plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/example-5921.json" "file_sha256" "c2fd8d995ff794cfc875bf9ad8e8575f8993b719755717eba6c2ab56785f7a78"}] "usage" {"e-5920" [] "q-71112" [] "e-5921" [] "q-71187" [] "e-5917" [] "q-129224" [] "q-71110" [] "q-129170" [] "q-71268" [] "q-129259" [] "q-71266" []} "basis" 1579 "retractions" [[17592186148145 :question/problem "Given the two planes $-x - 2y + z = 0$ and $2x + 4y - 2z + 1 = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n2. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n3. The planes are coincident."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146876 17592186146877 17592186146878 17592186146879 17592186146880 17592186146882 17592186146883 17592186146884 17592186146885 17592186146886 17592186146894 17592186146895 17592186146896 17592186146897 17592186146898 17592186309472 17592186309473 17592186309474 17592186309475 17592186309476 17592186309486 17592186309487 17592186309488 17592186309489 17592186309490 17592186309500 17592186309501 17592186309502 17592186309503 17592186309504 1759218